 ],
 "literal": "Rulers' hearts in, deeply to be imprinted, one poem also is / Rice also life is indeed not, water also life is indeed not / King is indeed the life of the wide-spread world / Therefore, 'I am the life' saying that should know / Spear-abundant army king's duty indeed.",
 "translation": "This is also a poem that must be deeply imprinted in the hearts of rulers: Rice is not the life, nor is water the life. The king is indeed the life of this wide, sprawling world! Therefore, to know that 'I am the life' is the duty of the king who leads an army abundant with spears.",
 "modern": "ஆட்சியாளர்களின் நெஞ்சங்களில் ஆழமாகப் பதியவேண்டிய ஒரு செய்யுளும் இதுவாகும்: நெல்லும் உயிர் அல்ல, நீரும் உயிர் அல்ல. பரந்த இடத்தைக் கொண்ட இவ்வுலகத்திற்கு மன்னனே உயிராவான். அதனால், 'நானே இவ்வுலகிற்கு உயிர்' என்பதை உணர்ந்து கொள்வது, வேல்கள் மிகுந்த படையைக் கொண்ட வேந்தனின் கடமையாகும்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is a famous piece of advice from the poet Mōcikīraṉār, classified under the theme of Poruṇmoḻik Kāñci in the Potuviyal tiṇai. The poet addresses the ruler, emphasizing that the true sustenance of a nation does not lie merely in material resources like grain or water, but in the righteous governance of the king. The wide-spread world depends entirely on the ruler for its life and order. Therefore, it is the ultimate duty of a king who commands a mighty army of spears to realize that he is the very soul and life of his people. This realization guides him to rule with deep responsibility and compassion.",
